mode: "100644"
text: "# Voice of the Customer — mine their actual words\n\nThis is the highest-value thing this skill produces. Copy that uses the audience's **own\nlanguage** for their problem feels like it gets them, and it outperforms copy written in the\nbrand's internal jargon — often dramatically. So instead of inventing how the audience talks, you\ncollect how they actually talk, and build a **language bank** the content skills draw from.\n\n> The rule: capture verbatim, don't paraphrase. The moment you \"clean up\" a customer's messy\n> real phrase into marketing-speak, you lose the exact thing that makes it resonate.\n\n## Where the real language lives\n\nMine whatever is available. Richest sources first:\n\n1. **Reviews** — the brand's *and competitors'*. Reviews are people describing problems and\n   desired outcomes in their own words. Competitor reviews reveal unmet needs and objections.\n2. **Social comments & replies** — on the brand's posts, competitors', and relevant big accounts\n   in the niche. Unfiltered reactions and questions.\n3. **Reddit, forums, niche communities, Discords** — where the audience talks to *each other*,\n   not to brands. The most honest language and the real questions.\n4. **Support tickets & FAQs** — the friction points, in the words people use when frustrated.\n5. **Sales-call notes & objections** — the buyer's hesitations, verbatim. Gold for B2B.\n6. **DMs & customer conversations** — direct, candid.\n7. **Search queries / \"people also ask\" / autocomplete** — exactly how they phrase what they\n   want.\n8. **Testimonials** — the transformation (before → after) in the customer's own words.\n\nUse what the user provides; if the agent can access public sources (reviews, subreddits,\ncomments), mine those. If little is available, say so and mark the language bank as provisional.\n\n## The method\n\n1. **Collect verbatim.** Pull real phrases — the messier and more specific, the better. Keep the\n   exact words.\n2. **Cluster** them into:\n   - **Problem language** — how they describe the pain (\"I never know if I'm doing it right\").\n   - **Desire language** — the outcome they want (\"I just want it done and off my plate\").\n   - **Objection language** — why they hesitate (\"switching sounds like a nightmare\").\n   - **Category language** — the words they use for the product/category (which may differ from\n     yours — \"the money stuff,\" not \"financial operations\").\n3. **Build the language bank** — an organized list of these real phrases.\n\n## Why mirroring wins\n\n- It signals understanding — \"they're describing my exact situation.\"\n- It lowers the comprehension cost — no translating your jargon into their reality.\n- It surfaces objections you can answer before they're voiced.\n- It often beats clever copy, because recognition beats cleverness.\n\nInternal jargon (\"streamline your financial operations\") is the opposite — it sounds like the\nbrand talking to itself. The audience says \"I'm drowning in receipts.\" Use *that*.\n\n## The language bank (what to hand downstream)\n\nA section in `audience.md` like:\n\n```\nLANGUAGE BANK\n- Problem: \"I don't even know where to start\", \"terrified I'll mess up taxes\", \"drowning in receipts\"\n- Desire: \"just want it done\", \"get my evenings back\", \"stop thinking about it\"\n- Objection: \"switching seems painful\", \"probably too expensive for me\", \"I'm not technical\"\n- They call it: \"the money stuff\", \"bookkeeping\", NOT \"financial operations\"\n```\n\nContent skills (especially `caption-writer` and `hook-writer`) pull hooks, pains, and CTAs\nstraight from this — so the brand sounds like it lives in the audience's world.\n\n## Honesty guardrail\n\nIf you couldn't find real evidence for a phrase, **don't put it in the bank as fact.** Either\nmark it \"(assumed — validate)\" or leave it out. A small bank of real phrases is worth more than a\nbig one of invented ones.\n"
